What I was looking to do was to link a device to print queue rather than 
linking a queue to a device. Sounds like HUH?

Normally, a print queue sends jobs to a specific device (printer), I don't
want that.
I want jobs to go to a queue and stay there. Then I want to be able to
assign
a device to that queue and allow it empty.

Why? We have 3 line printers and about 20 different forms we could print on
It would be nice to have people (who can't see what form in what printer)
just spool their jobs to a queue for that form. Then the person doing the
printing
when they load that form on whatever printer, have all jobs to that queue
(form)
print to that printer.

What was suggested:
   Setup the queues for each form to print to say: /dev/form/FORM
(/etc/printcap)
   With lpc setup those queues for queueing only, no printing (lpc start).
   When ready to print all jobs for a form, to
    ln -s /dev/printerdevice /dev/form/FORM
    lpc start queue FORM (now all the jobs will print)
    lpc stop queue FORM (when all forms have printed
    I would also have to use sudo to give the printing person access to
lpc.

Your Suggestion:
   setup queues for each form with the sd= and no lp= and create each dir
   lpr -Pprinter /usr/spool/FORM/df*
   lprm -PFORM -
   I would need sudo for printing person access to lpr /usr/spool & lprm
   I wouldn't want to change the permissions on the directory for security
reasons
      (wouldn't want people looking at other jobs that arn't theirs...)

Also suggested:
   To use LPRng (which I have downloaded, but haven't tried to compile
    and implement yet.

Any other ideas?? anyone??

