Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.osf.misc


Hello,

a network comprising about a dozen Alpha workstations:

mercury is NIS master, the others are NIS clients.

mars is exporting /home to all others using NFS.

On all workstations users can log in, find themselves in their home
directory /home/foo/bar, and 'ls -l' delivers the expected output: in
columns 3 and 4 they read their user and group name respectively.

But not on *mars*:

- Here users get numerical values (the UID and GID), and commands like
  'whoami' cause error messages (in this case 'Intruder alert'; cf.
  whoami(1) man page) that seem to indicate a NIS problem.

HOWEVER:

- There is *one* group whose members can log in and face no problems
  whatever. For them, the situation on mars is like it is on the other
  machines.

How can this be?

We have checked the obvious: The last line of /etc/passwd and /etc/group
consist of '+:' only.

Comparing the setup to that of the other NIS clients did not yield any
difference; the configuration via nissetup(8) is identical.

All workstations are running Digital UNIX 4.0a.

In our eyes the only difference between mars and the others is, that
mars mounts a RAID system as /home; the others use this via NFS.

Why does mars not trust any user *except those in that ``special'' group*?

Is it possible that mars is unable to interpret the information provided
from NIS master mercury properly?

Could it be there are files on mars that keep the system from updating
its NIS information thus leaving it in a state of ``deafness''?

The problem appeared out of the blue and could not be solved by
reconfiguring NIS nor by rebooting the machine.

We have no idea and are considering to put all users into the
``privileged'' group to enable them to work on mars again. :-/

Who can tell us where and what we have to search for?

Any hints will be greatly appreciated,
BtI aka #2

