It is quite simple, really. And mind you: There is actually no errors to the
filesystem, so it is a mere cosmetic problem.
If you place yourself in the top-level of the file-system in question (this
IS important), and issue either a [quotacheck -v .] or a [vquotacheck -v .]
you should be safe.
Please, be careful, not to be misled by the documentation: IF you issue a
plain quotacheck or vquotacheck -a (to check all filesystems mentioned in
/etc/fstab), you may end up with garbled readouts in df - I ended up with
NEGATIVE used space figures!
I guess that's the way you increase disk space nowadays *smile*
The above pertains to 3.2G and 4.0B.
Hope this helps you . . .
Kind regards,
Tusnelda

